  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the root-cause chain found in the source:\n\n**1. Where the error originates** \u2014 `pkg/registry/core/rest/storage_core.go`, `legacyProvider.PostStartHook()` (named `start-service-ip-repair-controllers`). It launches the ClusterIP and NodePort repair controllers and blocks on a `sync.WaitGroup` for both to report first success; if that doesn't happen within 1 minute, it returns `goerrors.New(\"unable to perform initial IP and Port allocation check\")`.\n\n**2. Propagation chain:**\n- `pkg/registry/core/service/ipallocator/controller/repairip.go` (`RepairIPAddress.RunUntil` \u2192 `runOnce` \u2192 `doRunOnce` \u2192 `syncService`) creates missing `IPAddress` objects via `client.NetworkingV1beta1().IPAddresses().Create(...)`.\n- That Create request goes through the generic admission chain. Two generic dispatchers gate *every* resource type (not just IPAddress-specific plugins): `staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/policy/generic/plugin.go` (ValidatingAdmissionPolicy) and `staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/webhook/generic/webhook.go` (webhook dispatch). Both call `WaitForReady()` and, on failure, return exactly `admission.NewForbidden(a, fmt.Errorf(\"not yet ready to handle request\"))` \u2014 the message in the ticket.\n- `WaitForReady()` (`staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/handler.go`) only waits `timeToWaitForReady = 10 * time.Second` before giving up if the plugin's own informer (policy source / webhook config source) hasn't synced.\n- Back in `repairip.go`, `doRunOnce()` propagates this Forbidden error up through `runOnce()`, which wraps calls in `retry.RetryOnConflict` (`staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/util/retry/util.go`) \u2014 that only retries `IsConflict` errors, so a Forbidden error is *not* retried and aborts the whole controller goroutine before `onFirstSuccess()`/`wg.Done()` ever fires.\n- Because `wg.Wait()` never completes, the PostStartHook times out after 1 minute and returns an error. `staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/server/hooks.go`'s `runPostStartHook` treats any non-nil hook error as fatal: `klog.Fatalf(...)` \u2014 crashing the apiserver process, not just failing readyz.\n- Meanwhile `/readyz` reports both `poststarthook/start-service-ip-repair-controllers` (via `postStartHookHealthz` in `hooks.go`) and `informer-sync` (`staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/server/healthz/healthz.go`) as failing, matching the ticket's readyz output.\n\n**3. The race:** `RepairIPAddress.RunUntil` only waits for its own narrow informer set (Services, ServiceCIDRs, IPAddresses) plus the default `ServiceCIDR` object (created by the separate `start-kubernetes-service-cidr-controller` hook in `pkg/controlplane/instance.go` / `pkg/controlplane/controller/defaultservicecidr/default_servicecidr_controller.go`) before it starts writing. But the admission-layer gates on those very writes depend on *different* informers (webhook/policy configuration sources) with only a 10s grace period. During a rolling upgrade the extra load/latency on a mixed-version cluster can push those admission informers past 10s while the repair controller's narrower set has already synced and starts creating IPAddress objects \u2014 producing a non-retryable Forbidden that aborts the repair controller, which after 1 minute fatally crashes the apiserver via the PostStartHook. Once all nodes are on v1.33 the timing pressure eases and the race stops reproducing.",
